        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 月饼",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "月饼 — yuèbǐng — \"mooncake\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我买了两个月饼 wǒ mǎi le liǎng ge yuèbǐng — I bought two mooncakes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 饼 (bǐng) — nine strokes: on the left the food piece of 饭, three strokes; on the right two short strokes, two bars, and two strokes down. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "月饼 (yuèbǐng) is built from 月 'the moon' and 饼 (bǐng) 'a cake', both inherited from Middle Chinese, ngjwot and pjiengX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which gives the second only its reading."
            }
          ]
        },
